# Settings of /etc/grafana/grafana.ini on the OpenCloud VM (Grafana 9.1.6, installed from
# grafana-enterprise_9.1.6_amd64.deb). Checked on the live server 2026-09-24.
# Only these lines differ from the packaged defaults; everything else stays commented out (";"),
# so Grafana used its defaults (HTTP port 3000, sqlite /var/lib/grafana/grafana.db).

[auth.anonymous]
# Public, read-only access to dashboards without login.
enabled = true
org_name = Humedata ISF (Atlas beta)
org_role = Viewer

[feature_highlights]
enabled = false

# SMTP was NOT configured on the server (the [smtp] section stayed at its defaults).
# The manual describes how to enable it; pattern, if ever needed:
# [smtp]
# enabled = true
# host = <SMTP_HOST>:587
# user = <SMTP_USER>
# password = <SMTP_PASSWORD>      ; wrap in """...""" if it contains # or ;
# from_address = admin@grafana.localhost
# from_name = Grafana

# Everything else lived in grafana.db (backed up privately, see ../README.md "Where the data lives"):
#   orgs:        1 "Humedat@ brewer " (admin org), 3 "Humedata ISF (Atlas beta)" (anonymous viewers land here)
#   datasources: "MySQL" in both orgs -> localhost:3306, database mqtt;
#                org 1 connects as root (!), org 3 as grafanareader (read-only)
#   dashboards:  exported to dashboards/*.json (7), annotations to annotations.json (102, 2023-09 -> 2024-06)
#   images:      custom logos/icons in img/ were copied to /usr/share/grafana/public/img/ and used from
#                Markdown text panels as /public/img/<file>
